menu "CV-X-IF integration configuration"
	depends on CORE_CV32E40X_UPSTREAM || CORE_CVA6_UPSTREAM || CORE_CV32E40PX

	config CVXIF_NON_SPECULATIVE
		bool "Commit-gated admission (admit only once non-speculative)"
		default n
		help
			Speculative admission is the default: admit at the issue
			handshake and hold results until commit. It both pipelines
			the ISAX (~2.4-2.5x offload throughput) and drops the
			commit-gated input buffer from the glue.

			Set this to force the old commit-gated admission, which
			serialises ISAX execution (max 1 in the datapath) but needs
			no rollback. An ISAX with custom registers falls back to it
			automatically -- a killed entry is dropped and there is no
			way to undo its register writes -- so this switch is only
			needed to force the fallback for an ISAX without them.

	config CVXIF_IN_ORDER_RESULT
		bool "In-order result emission (stage results to the last stage)"
		default n
		help
			By default each result is handed over at the stage that
			produces it, and the entry retires there; stages arbitrate
			for the single result port (deepest = oldest first) and a
			loser stalls in place. That removes the 32-bit staging
			registers that used to drag every result to the last stage.

			Set this to force the old in-order emission. Needed only if
			the host cannot accept results out of order -- CV32E40X can
			(fpu_ss runs OUT_OF_ORDER=1 on it). An ISAX that still
			observes the interface after its WrRD falls back on its own.

	config CVXIF_INPUT_BUFFER
		bool "Keep the issue -> stage-0 input buffer"
		default n
		help
			By default the glue admits in the issue cycle, without the
			staging register between the issue handshake and stage 0.
			Set this to keep the buffer: it decouples the core's ID
			stage from the ISAX pipeline (non-ISAX instructions behind
			an ISAX one keep flowing while stage 0 is occupied) at the
			cost of ~125 register bits and a cycle of latency. The
			commit-gated build always keeps it -- there the buffer IS
			the wait for the commit.

	config CVXIF_ALLOW_CUSTOM_REGS
		bool "CVA6: allow ISAXes with custom registers despite commit_kill=0"
		depends on CORE_CVA6_UPSTREAM
		default n
		help
			CVA6 hardwires commit.commit_kill = 0, so an ISAX instruction
			offloaded in the shadow of a trapping instruction executes
			twice. Stateless ISAXes are unaffected; ISAXes with custom
			registers are corrupted, so the CVA6 wrapper refuses them
			unless this is set. See docs/cvxif.md.

	config CVXIF_GEN_PROTOCOL_TB
		bool "Also emit the self-checking CV-X-IF protocol testbench"
		default n
		help
			Emits tb_cvxif_glue_<name>.sv, a standalone protocol testbench
			driving the glue against the ISAX's Python golden model. This
			is the only place kill-mid-datapath and the result.rd/we
			fields are exercised (the cores kill within one cycle of
			issue and derive rd themselves).
endmenu
